==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|silken}}, {{m|enm|selken}}, {{m|enm|seolkene}}, from {{inh|en|ang|seolcen}}, {{m|ang|seolucen||made of silk, silken}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|silk|en|t2=made of}}. Cognate with Scots {{m|sco|selkin}}, {{m|sco|silkin||silken}}, Icelandic {{m|is|silki||silken}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-silken.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Made of [[silk]].
#: ''a '''silken''' veil''
# Having a [[smooth]], [[soft]], or [[light]] [[texture]], like that of silk; [[suggestive]] of silk.
#* '''1646''', {{w|Richard Crashaw}}, “Vpon Mr. ''Staninough’s'' Death” in ''Steps to the Temple: Sacred Poems, with Other Delights of the Muses'', London: Humphrey Moseley, p. 40,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A34930.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Come then youth, Beauty, and Blood, all ye soft powers,
#*: Whose '''silken''' flatteryes swell a few fond houres.
#* '''1792''', {{w|Mary Wollstonecraft}}, ''{{w|A Vindication of the Rights of Woman}}'', London: J. Johnson, Part I, Chapter 9, p. 322,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004903441.0001.000]</sup>
#*: {{...}} love is not to be bought, in any sense of the words, its '''silken''' wings are instantly shrivelled up when any thing beside a return in kind is sought.
#* '''1855''', {{w|Elizabeth Gaskell}}, ''{{w|North and South (Gaskell novel)|North and South}}'', Chapter 1,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/4276/4276-h/4276-h.htm]</sup>
#*: {{...}} in spite of the buzz in the next room, Edith had rolled herself up into a soft ball of muslin and ribbon, and '''silken''' curls, and gone off into a peaceful little after-dinner nap.
#* '''1994''', {{w|Stephen Fry}}, ''{{w|The Hippopotamus}}'', Random House, 2010, Chapter 2, p. 37,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=2vV4vau4818C&printsec=frontcover]</sup>
#*: He heard the '''silken''' rustle of a dressing-gown being drawn on.
# {{lb|en|figuratively|of speech, singing, [[oratory]], etc.}} [[smooth|Smooth]]ly [[utter]]ed; [[flowing]], [[subtle]], or [[convincing]] in presentation.
#* {{circa|1594}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Love's Labour's Lost|Love’s Labour’s Lost}}'', Act V, Scene 2,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=loveslabours&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: Taffeta phrases, '''silken''' terms precise,
#*: Three-piled hyperboles, spruce affectation,
#*: Figures pedantical; these summer-flies
#*: Have blown me full of maggot ostentation:
# Dressed in silk.
#* {{circa|1596}} {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|King John (play)|King John}}'', Act V, Scene 1,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=kingjohn&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]</sup>
#*: {{...}} shall a beardless boy,
#*: A cocker’d '''silken''' wanton, brave our fields {{...}}?
#* '''1633''', {{w|John Donne}}, “Satyre I” in ''Poems'', London: John Marriot, p. 327,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A69225.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Yet though he cannot skip forth now to greet
#*: Every fine '''silken''' painted foole we meet,
#*: He then to him with amorous smiles allures,
#* '''1724''', {{w|Aaron Hill (writer)|Aaron Hill}}, ''The Plain Dealer'', London: S. Richardson & A. Wilde, 1730, Volume 2, No. 81, 28 December, 1724, p. 197,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004891141.0001.002]</sup>
#*: Last ''Saturday'' was three Weeks, at Two, in the Afternoon, I sent out my Servant, to watch a Couple of these '''''Silken''' Strollers'', and keep, if possible, within ''[[ken|Ken]]'' of them.
#* '''1968''', {{w|Jan Morris}}, ''Pax Britannica: The Climax of Empire'', London: Faber & Faber, 2010, Chapter 10, p. 200,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=pgftEXaDykkC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: {{...}} the Viceroy moved magnificently through India, resplendent with all the colour and dash of the vast Empire at his feet, with his superb bodyguard jangling scarlet beside his carriage, '''silken''' Indian princes bowing at his carpet, generals quivering at the salute and ceremonial salutes of thirty-one guns {{...}}

